insert into dnew values
(1, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'A ventilated patient with severe pulmonary hypertension suddenly drops BP to 70/40 with a rising CVP after a large tidal volume increase. The most likely mechanism is:', '["Tension pneumothorax from barotrauma, shifting the mediastinum to the right", "Vasodilation from deep sedation, causing a distributive type of shock", "Raised RV afterload from high intrathoracic pressure, causing acute RV failure", "Left ventricular failure from fluid overload, causing acute pulmonary oedema"]'::jsonb, 2, 'High airway pressures compress pulmonary vessels and raise RV afterload. A failing RV dilates, shifts the septum and drops cardiac output, with a rising CVP.', 'Respiratory'),
(2, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'In a patient with pulmonary arterial hypertension, which factor most worsens pulmonary vascular resistance and must be avoided in the ICU?', '["Normothermia with adequate analgesia", "Hypoxia, hypercapnia and acidosis", "A slightly alkalotic pH from mild hyperventilation", "Mild hyperoxia with normocapnia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hypoxia, hypercarbia and acidosis cause pulmonary vasoconstriction. Oxygen, normocapnia and avoiding acidosis lower PVR.', 'Respiratory'),
(3, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'Inhaled nitric oxide is used in ICU patients with acute pulmonary hypertension because it:', '["Dilates pulmonary vessels in ventilated lung with little systemic effect", "Increases right ventricular contractility directly as an inotrope", "Reduces blood viscosity and platelet adhesion, preventing thrombosis", "Dilates systemic arteries more than pulmonary arteries, lowering afterload"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Inhaled NO acts locally on ventilated alveoli and is rapidly inactivated by haemoglobin, so systemic hypotension is minimal. Methaemoglobin is monitored.', 'Respiratory'),
(4, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'A patient with chronic pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure needs fluid management. The ICU nurse should expect:', '["Small, careful fluid boluses, as the RV tolerates under- and over-filling poorly", "Large crystalloid boluses to raise preload and push blood through the lungs", "Fluids guided only by hourly urine output, ignoring CVP and the echo", "Strict fluid restriction regardless of the blood pressure or the CVP"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An over-distended RV shifts the septum and reduces LV filling; too little preload drops output. Small, monitored boluses are safest.', 'Respiratory'),
(5, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'Thoracotomy is generally indicated for massive haemothorax when drainage exceeds:', '[">2500ml in total, regardless of the rate", ">500ml in total or >100ml/hr for 1 hour", ">1500ml or >200ml/hr for 3 hours", ">1000ml in total or any bright red blood"]'::jsonb, 2, 'These specific volume thresholds indicate ongoing significant bleeding that chest tube drainage alone won''t control.', 'Respiratory'),
(6, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'After a chest drain is inserted for haemothorax, the initial drainage is 1200 mL and the patient stabilises. The nurse should next:', '["Clamp the drain for one hour to allow the blood to clot", "Remove the drain once the BP has been stable for 1 hour", "Record hourly drainage; report over 200 mL/hour for 2–4 hours", "Milk and strip the tube vigorously every 15 minutes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ongoing loss over 200 mL/hour for 2–4 hours, or an initial 1500 mL, suggests the need for thoracotomy. Clamping risks tension haemothorax.', 'Respiratory'),
(7, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'About the x ray features of haemothorax', '["An erect film is less sensitive than a supine film", "It is seen only when more than 1500 mL of blood is present", "A supine film shows a diffuse haze over the affected hemithorax", "Costophrenic blunting appears with as little as 20 mL"]'::jsonb, 2, 'On a supine film, blood spreads out and causes general increased opacification rather than a clear costophrenic blunting.', 'Respiratory'),
(8, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'A patient with a left haemothorax drained by chest tube becomes hypotensive and the drainage suddenly stops. The nurse should first suspect:', '["Air leak from the underwater seal causing pneumothorax", "Vasovagal reaction to the presence of the chest drain", "That the bleeding has stopped and the lung is re-expanded", "A clotted or kinked tube with blood re-accumulating in the chest"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Sudden cessation with worsening haemodynamics suggests blockage. Check the tubing, assess breath sounds and inform the surgeon urgently.', 'Respiratory'),
(9, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'Autotransfusion from a chest drain collection system is most appropriate when:', '["A known malignant pleural effusion with recent bleeding", "Blood that has been in the collection chamber over 24 hours", "A penetrating injury that has also perforated the bowel", "A large, uncontaminated haemothorax within a few hours of injury"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Fresh, uncontaminated blood can be returned via a filter. Old or contaminated blood (bowel injury, infection, malignancy) must not be reinfused.', 'Respiratory'),
(10, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'Which chest X-ray finding most strongly suggests blunt traumatic aortic injury?', '["A widened mediastinum with loss of the aortic knob", "A small apical pneumothorax on the left side", "Fractured lower ribs on the right side only", "Blunting of one costophrenic angle with no shift"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Other signs include tracheal shift to the right, depression of the left main bronchus and an apical cap. CT angiography confirms.', 'Respiratory'),
(11, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'For a patient with suspected traumatic aortic injury awaiting repair, the ICU nurse should aim to keep:', '["Large fluid boluses running continuously to prevent any hypotension", "HR under 100 and systolic BP about 100–120 using beta-blockade", "Heart rate above 120 per minute to keep the cardiac output high", "Systolic BP above 160 mmHg to maintain cerebral and renal flow"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Lowering shear stress (dP/dt) with esmolol or labetalol reduces the risk of free rupture before surgery or stenting.', 'Respiratory'),
(12, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'The most common site of blunt traumatic aortic injury is the:', '["Abdominal aorta, at the level of the renal arteries", "Aortic root, just above the aortic valve", "Aortic arch, at the origin of the brachiocephalic trunk", "Aortic isthmus, just beyond the left subclavian"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The isthmus is fixed by the ligamentum arteriosum; sudden deceleration tears the vessel there.', 'Respiratory'),
(13, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'Which mechanism of injury should make the nurse suspect blunt aortic trauma?', '["A minor rear-end collision at about 10 km/h", "A stab wound to the thigh with brisk bleeding", "A low-speed fall from standing height in an elderly patient", "High-speed deceleration, e.g. head-on crash or fall from height"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Sudden deceleration creates shearing forces at the fixed isthmus.', 'Respiratory'),
(14, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'Shock lung is another name for:', '["ARDS developing after shock or major trauma", "Pulmonary oedema caused by left ventricular failure", "Lung collapse caused by a large pleural effusion", "Bronchospasm triggered by a blood transfusion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Shock, sepsis and trauma trigger diffuse alveolar damage with non-cardiogenic oedema and refractory hypoxaemia.', 'Respiratory'),
(15, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'Which finding distinguishes ARDS (shock lung) from cardiogenic pulmonary oedema?', '["Unilateral infiltrates with a large heart shadow", "Rapid improvement with furosemide alone", "Raised wedge pressure above 25 mmHg", "Normal wedge pressure with bilateral infiltrates"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In ARDS the left atrial pressure is not the cause; PAWP is usually 18 mmHg or less, and hypoxaemia is refractory to oxygen.', 'Respiratory'),
(16, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'A haemopneumothorax on an erect chest X-ray typically shows:', '["Diffuse ground-glass shadowing", "A deep sulcus sign only", "A curved meniscus without any air", "A straight horizontal air-fluid level"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Air above blood produces a flat fluid level. A simple effusion produces a curved meniscus.', 'Respiratory'),
(17, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'On a supine trauma chest X-ray, a pneumothorax may be suggested by:', '["Kerley B lines", "A curved meniscus", "A deep sulcus sign", "A clear apical lung edge"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In the supine position air collects anteriorly and inferiorly, deepening the costophrenic angle.', 'Respiratory'),
(18, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'Massive haemothorax is defined by an initial drainage of at least:', '["500 mL", "3000 mL", "1500 mL", "200 mL"]'::jsonb, 2, 'It also includes ongoing loss of more than 200 mL/hour; such patients usually need thoracotomy.', 'Respiratory'),
(19, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'A ventilated patient with pulmonary hypertension needs suctioning. The best approach is:', '["Pre-oxygenate and use short, closed suction to avoid hypoxia", "Avoid suction completely to protect the pulmonary vessels", "Disconnect the ventilator and bag with room air", "Long open suction to clear all secretions at once"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Desaturation and agitation raise PVR and can precipitate RV failure.', 'Respiratory'),
(20, 'Pulmonary Hypertension, Haemothorax, Chest and Aortic Trauma', 'Sildenafil is used in pulmonary arterial hypertension because it:', '["Acts as a potent loop diuretic that reduces right ventricular preload", "Inhibits phosphodiesterase-5, increasing NO-mediated vasodilation", "Blocks endothelin receptors on the pulmonary vascular smooth muscle", "Acts as a beta-blocker that slows the heart and reduces RV workload"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bosentan is the endothelin antagonist. Nitrates must not be given with sildenafil because of severe hypotension.', 'Respiratory'),
(21, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'A 70 kg man after a crash has pulse 125, BP 95/70, RR 32, urine 10 mL/hour and is anxious and confused. Using the ATLS classes, his estimated blood loss is:', '["Class IV, over 2000 mL (over 40%)", "Class I, under 750 mL (under 15%)", "Class II, 750–1500 mL (15–30%)", "Class III, 1500–2000 mL (30–40%)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Class III shows a falling systolic BP, tachycardia above 120, low urine output and confusion. It requires blood transfusion.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(22, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'Which sign appears earliest in Class II haemorrhage?', '["A narrowed pulse pressure with tachycardia", "Anuria for more than 2 hours", "A systolic BP below 90 mmHg", "Loss of consciousness and confusion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Diastolic pressure rises from vasoconstriction, so the pulse pressure narrows before systolic BP falls.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(23, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'A trauma patient on beta-blockers loses 1.5 L of blood. Why may the class of haemorrhage be underestimated?', '["They cause a diuresis that masks oliguria", "They blunt the tachycardic response", "They keep the patient alert despite shock", "They raise the BP and so hide hypotension"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Elderly patients, athletes and those on beta-blockers or pacemakers may not show tachycardia.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(24, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'The ''golden hour'' in trauma emphasises:', '["Delaying surgery until the patient is fully stable", "Waiting one hour before any IV fluid is given", "A full secondary survey before any treatment", "Early assessment, resuscitation and definitive care"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Early control of haemorrhage and airway reduces deaths in the second peak of trauma mortality.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(25, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'In the trimodal distribution of trauma deaths, the second peak (minutes to hours) is mainly due to:', '["Pulmonary embolism occurring after hospital discharge", "Haemorrhage, airway and chest injuries that are treatable", "Sepsis and multi-organ failure several weeks later", "Immediate brain stem and aortic transection injuries"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is the target of ATLS and the golden hour.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(26, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'The six phases of pre-hospital trauma care begin with:', '["Rehabilitation and follow-up", "Detection and reporting of the incident", "The secondary survey in the ED", "Hospital admission and surgery"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Phases include detection, reporting, response, on-scene care, care in transit and transfer to definitive care.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(27, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'Permissive hypotension in penetrating torso trauma means:', '["Allowing the BP to fall to any level without any treatment", "Large crystalloid boluses to bring the BP back to normal", "Vasopressors as first-line treatment instead of giving blood", "A lower BP target (systolic 80–90) until bleeding is controlled"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It limits dilution of clotting factors and disruption of clots. It is avoided in head injury, where cerebral perfusion is needed.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(28, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'Damage control resuscitation in massive haemorrhage from polytrauma emphasises:', '["Avoiding surgical bleeding control until the patient is fully stabilised", "Early balanced blood products, permissive hypotension and bleeding control", "Aggressive large-volume crystalloid infusion as the sole resuscitation strategy", "Delaying all blood products until the haemoglobin falls below 5 g/dL"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Damage control resuscitation favours early, balanced transfusion of blood products in ratios approximating whole blood, limits crystalloid to avoid dilutional coagulopathy, and pursues rapid haemorrhage source control rather than prolonged pre-operative stabilisation.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(29, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'The ''lethal triad'' in polytrauma refers to:', '["Anaemia, hypokalaemia and hyponatraemia", "Hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy", "Tachycardia, tachypnoea and fever", "Hypoxia, hypercapnia and hypotension"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each worsens the others. Warming, early blood products and haemorrhage control break the cycle.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(30, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'Tranexamic acid in major trauma is most effective when given:', '["Only once the INR exceeds 3", "After 8 hours of injury", "Only after surgery is completed", "Within 3 hours of injury"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The CRASH-2 trial showed benefit within 3 hours and possible harm after.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(31, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'Diagnostic peritoneal lavage is considered positive in blunt trauma when the aspirate shows:', '["A lavage amylase within the normal laboratory range", "White cells below 500/mm³ with no bacteria seen", "10 mL or more of frank blood, or over 100 000 RBC/mm³", "Clear lavage fluid with a few red cells on microscopy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Other positive findings include WBC over 500/mm³, bile, bacteria or food fibres.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(32, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'Before diagnostic peritoneal lavage, the nurse should ensure:', '["A full bladder is maintained to lift the bowel up", "The patient has eaten recently to stimulate the bowel", "The patient is placed prone for the procedure", "The bladder is catheterised and the stomach decompressed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Decompression reduces the risk of puncturing the bladder or stomach during catheter insertion.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(33, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'FAST has largely replaced DPL in unstable trauma patients because FAST is:', '["Rapid, non-invasive, repeatable and done at the bedside", "Free of operator dependence", "Able to show solid organ grading in detail", "More sensitive for hollow viscus injury than any test"]'::jsonb, 0, 'FAST detects free fluid in the pericardium, Morison''s pouch, splenorenal space and pelvis. It misses some bowel and retroperitoneal injuries.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(34, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'In FAST, the hepatorenal space (Morison''s pouch) is examined because:', '["It shows a pneumothorax more clearly than any other view", "It is the most dependent space where fluid collects when supine", "It is the window used to measure the bladder volume", "It gives the clearest view of the heart and pericardium"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Free fluid in Morison''s pouch strongly suggests haemoperitoneum in trauma.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(35, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'A bladder scan in an ICU patient with a urinary catheter showing 450 mL suggests:', '["Acute kidney injury with oliguria", "Normal residual volume in a catheterised patient", "Catheter blockage or malposition", "Over-hydration requiring diuretics"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A catheterised bladder should be nearly empty. Check for kinks, clots or a balloon in the urethra.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(36, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'Before concluding a patient is oliguric from acute kidney injury, the ICU nurse should:', '["Bladder-scan or flush the catheter to exclude obstruction", "Restrict all IV fluids immediately to avoid overload", "Give furosemide 80 mg IV to test the kidneys", "Start preparing the patient for urgent dialysis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Post-renal obstruction is a simple, reversible cause of low urine output.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(37, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'During central venous catheter insertion via the subclavian route, the patient is positioned:', '["Prone, with the head turned away from the site", "Left lateral, with the knees flexed to the chest", "Head-down, to distend the vein and prevent air embolism", "Sitting upright at 90 degrees with the arms by the side"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Head-down tilt raises venous pressure. A chest X-ray afterwards checks tip position and excludes pneumothorax.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(38, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'During pericardiocentesis, the ECG shows ST elevation and ventricular ectopics. This suggests:', '["The needle is touching the myocardium and must be withdrawn", "The tamponade has resolved and the heart is refilling", "A normal response to the injected local anaesthetic", "The effusion has been successfully and fully drained"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Myocardial contact causes injury current and arrhythmias; the needle is pulled back.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(39, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'Beck''s triad in cardiac tamponade after chest trauma consists of:', '["Tachycardia, fever and pericardial rub", "Hypotension, raised JVP and muffled heart sounds", "Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular breathing", "Hypotension, low JVP and loud heart sounds"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pulsus paradoxus (systolic fall above 10 mmHg on inspiration) is another sign.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(40, 'Haemorrhage Classes, Polytrauma, Golden Hour and Trauma Procedures', 'A polytrauma patient develops confusion, hypoxia and a petechial rash over the axillae 48 hours after femoral fracture. The likely diagnosis is:', '["Transfusion-related lung injury", "Pulmonary thromboembolism", "Septic shock from wound infection", "Fat embolism syndrome"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Fat globules from long-bone marrow cause respiratory, neurological and skin features. Early fracture fixation reduces risk.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(41, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'According to the Monro-Kellie doctrine, once compensatory mechanisms (CSF displacement, venous compression) are exhausted, further small increases in intracranial volume cause what?', '["A linear, predictable rise in ICP", "No change in ICP", "A decrease in ICP", "Sharp, exponential rises in ICP"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Once the brain''s limited compensatory reserve is depleted, the ICP-volume curve becomes steep, so even small additional volume increases cause disproportionately large, exponential rises in ICP.', 'Neurology'),
(42, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'Per the Monro-Kellie doctrine, which two components are displaced early to compensate for a growing intracranial mass lesion?', '["CSF into the spinal space and venous blood out of the skull", "Arterial blood only, by constriction of the cerebral arteries", "Brain tissue only, by compression of the normal cortex", "Bone, by remodelling and expansion of the skull sutures"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Early compensation for a growing mass occurs through displacement of CSF into the spinal subarachnoid space and reduction of intracranial venous blood volume, before ICP begins to rise significantly.', 'Neurology'),
(43, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'What is considered the normal range for brain tissue oxygen tension (PbtO2) measured by a Licox monitor?', '["100-150 mmHg", "5-10 mmHg", "60-100 mmHg", "20-40 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Normal PbtO2 is generally considered to be approximately 20-40 mmHg; values below this range suggest inadequate cerebral tissue oxygenation.', 'Neurology'),
(44, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'Where is the Licox brain tissue oxygen monitoring catheter typically placed?', '["Peripheral muscle tissue of the thigh", "The subarachnoid space over the spine", "The ventricle, solely for CSF sampling", "Uninjured white matter, often frontal"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Licox catheter is inserted into uninjured (or at-risk penumbral) white matter, commonly in the frontal lobe, to monitor local tissue oxygenation rather than global brain function.', 'Neurology'),
(45, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'An external ventricular drain (EVD) that is leveled too LOW relative to the reference point will most likely cause what?', '["Over-drainage of CSF, risking ventricular collapse", "Immediate infection of the ventricular catheter", "Under-drainage of CSF, with a rising ICP", "No effect, because drainage depends on ICP only"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A drainage chamber positioned too low relative to the reference point creates excessive siphoning pressure, leading to over-drainage, ventricular collapse, and risk of rebleeding.', 'Neurology'),
(46, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'According to the Monro-Kellie doctrine, which component can the ICU nurse most directly influence to reduce ICP?', '["Cerebral blood volume, via head-up position, neck alignment, PaCO₂ control", "CSF volume, by encouraging the patient to cough strongly", "Brain tissue volume, by giving large volumes of IV fluids", "Skull volume, by positioning the patient completely flat"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Head-up 30°, neutral neck, loose tube ties and normocapnia reduce venous and arterial blood volume.', 'Neurology'),
(47, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'A Licox reading of PbtO₂ 12 mmHg with normal ICP and MAP indicates:', '["Over-drainage from the external ventricular drain", "Hyperoxia, so the FiO₂ should be reduced at once", "Brain tissue hypoxia; review oxygenation, Hb and perfusion", "Normal brain tissue oxygenation needing no action"]'::jsonb, 2, 'PbtO₂ below 15–20 mmHg is critical. Causes include hypoxaemia, anaemia, low CPP and vasospasm.', 'Neurology'),
(48, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'The transducer of an external ventricular drain is zeroed at the level of the:', '["Phlebostatic axis at the 4th intercostal space", "Sternal notch", "Tragus of the ear (foramen of Monro)", "Iliac crest"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The tragus approximates the foramen of Monro. Re-level after every position change.', 'Neurology'),
(49, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'Before moving a patient with an open external ventricular drain, the nurse should:', '["Disconnect the drain from the bag for the transfer", "Clamp per protocol, then re-level and reopen after the move", "Lower the drip chamber to the floor during the move", "Leave it open while sitting the patient upright"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Moving an open drain can cause sudden over- or under-drainage.', 'Neurology'),
(50, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'An EVD set at 15 cmH₂O above the tragus means CSF drains when:', '["Whenever the patient coughs only", "ICP exceeds about 15 cmH₂O", "ICP falls below 15 cmH₂O", "The patient''s BP exceeds 15 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The height of the drip chamber sets the pressure that must be overcome before CSF drains.', 'Neurology'),
(51, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'A sudden increase in clear EVD drainage with a falling GCS and headache on sitting up suggests:', '["Cerebral vasospasm after subarachnoid bleeding", "A normal response to sitting the patient upright", "A blocked drain with a steadily rising ICP", "Over-drainage causing low ICP or subdural collection"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Excessive drainage can collapse ventricles and tear bridging veins.', 'Neurology'),
(52, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'During targeted temperature management, shivering should be prevented because it:', '["It improves the accuracy of the temperature probes", "It reduces cerebral oxygen demand and so is beneficial", "It raises metabolic rate and O₂ use, opposing neuroprotection", "It lowers the core temperature far too quickly"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sedation, analgesia, magnesium, counter-warming and sometimes paralysis control shivering.', 'Neurology'),
(53, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'During the cooling phase of induced hypothermia, the nurse should monitor closely for:', '["Tachycardia, high potassium and low blood glucose", "Bradycardia, low potassium, high glucose, coagulopathy", "Polycythaemia, hypercalcaemia and hypernatraemia", "Hypertension with a shortened bleeding time only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cold shifts potassium into cells; rewarming shifts it out, risking hyperkalaemia.', 'Neurology'),
(54, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'Rewarming after targeted temperature management should be:', '["Stopped at 33 °C permanently", "Rapid, 2–3 °C per hour", "As fast as possible with hot fluids", "Slow, about 0.25–0.5 °C per hour"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Rapid rewarming causes vasodilation, hypotension, rebound hyperkalaemia and raised ICP.', 'Neurology'),
(55, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'Guillain-Barré syndrome patients in ICU need serial measurement of:', '["Arterial lactate and pH every hour", "Serum calcium and magnesium daily", "Peak expiratory flow rate every 4 hours", "Vital capacity and negative inspiratory force"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A falling vital capacity below about 15–20 mL/kg indicates the need for ventilation.', 'Neurology'),
(56, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'Autonomic instability in Guillain-Barré syndrome may present as:', '["Constant bradycardia with a low temperature", "Swings in BP and heart rate, with arrhythmias", "Hypoglycaemia with recurrent seizures", "Hyperkalaemia with peaked T waves"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Continuous cardiac monitoring is required; suction or turning can trigger bradycardia.', 'Neurology'),
(57, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'The specific treatments that shorten the course of Guillain-Barré syndrome are:', '["Pyridostigmine and atropine", "Dantrolene and cooling", "IV immunoglobulin or plasma exchange", "High-dose steroids and antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Steroids are not effective in GBS.', 'Neurology'),
(58, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'Critical illness polyneuropathy differs from Guillain-Barré syndrome in that it:', '["It follows a diarrhoeal illness, with demyelination", "It always affects the facial muscles first", "It causes high CSF protein with no other cause", "It develops in sepsis and MOF, with axonal loss"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It contributes to difficult weaning and ICU-acquired weakness.', 'Neurology'),
(59, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'A key risk factor for ICU-acquired weakness that the nurse can reduce is:', '["Early mobilisation and physiotherapy", "Tight glucose control below 10 mmol/L", "Daily sedation holds with SBTs", "Prolonged immobility and deep sedation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Sepsis, steroids, neuromuscular blockers and hyperglycaemia also contribute.', 'Neurology'),
(60, 'Neuro-monitoring: Monro-Kellie, Licox, EVD, Induced Hypothermia, Neuropathies', 'The Medical Research Council (MRC) sum score is used to:', '["Grade muscle strength and diagnose ICU-AW", "Measure the depth of sedation hourly", "Assess delirium in ventilated patients", "Predict ICU mortality on admission"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A sum score below 48/60 indicates ICU-acquired weakness.', 'Neurology'),
(61, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'Intra-abdominal hypertension is:', '["When IAP is consistently \\>12 mmHg with organ dysfunction", "When IAP is \\>12 mmHg without organ dysfunction", "When IAP is consistently \\<12 mmHg with organ dysfunction", "When IAP is consistently \\> 5 mmHg without organ dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Intra-abdominal hypertension is defined as sustained IAP >12mmHg WITHOUT necessarily needing organ dysfunction to be present.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(62, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'The following is a secondary abdominal compartment syndrome.', '["Ascites", "Septic shock", "Pancreatitis", "Trauma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Septic shock (a non-abdominal originating cause) is a classic secondary cause of abdominal compartment syndrome.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(63, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'Intra-abdominal pressure is measured via the bladder with the transducer zeroed at the:', '["The symphysis pubis, during a deep inspiration", "Mid-axillary line at the iliac crest, supine, end-expiration", "Phlebostatic axis, 4th intercostal space, at end-inspiration", "The umbilicus, with the patient sitting up at 45 degrees"]'::jsonb, 1, 'No more than 25 mL of saline is instilled. Abdominal muscle contraction must be absent.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(64, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'Abdominal perfusion pressure (APP) is calculated as:', '["Intra-abdominal pressure minus CVP", "Diastolic pressure plus intra-abdominal pressure", "Mean arterial pressure minus intra-abdominal pressure", "Systolic pressure minus intra-abdominal pressure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An APP of at least 60 mmHg is usually targeted.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(65, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'Organ effects of abdominal compartment syndrome include:', '["Hypertension with increased venous return", "Improved lung compliance and diuresis", "Polyuria, low airway pressures, high cardiac output", "Oliguria, high airway pressures, low cardiac output"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Raised IAP compresses the kidneys and IVC and pushes the diaphragm up.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(66, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'Definitive treatment of abdominal compartment syndrome with organ failure is:', '["Surgical decompressive laparotomy", "Oral laxatives and enemas", "High-dose diuretics alone", "Increasing PEEP to splint the abdomen"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Non-surgical measures (sedation, NG decompression, fluid balance) are tried first; persistent ACS needs decompression.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(67, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'Stress ulcer prophylaxis in ICU is clearly indicated in a patient who:', '["With no bleeding risk factors and a short stay", "Ventilated for over 48 hours, or with a coagulopathy", "Taking low-dose aspirin only, with no other risks", "Eating a full diet after minor uncomplicated surgery"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Other indications include shock, severe burns, head injury and high-dose steroids.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(68, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'A disadvantage of prolonged acid suppression for stress ulcer prophylaxis is an increased risk of:', '["Ventilator-associated pneumonia and C. difficile", "Upper GI bleeding from rebound acid", "Peptic ulcer perforation and peritonitis", "Hypercalcaemia and kidney stones"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Higher gastric pH allows bacterial overgrowth. Prophylaxis is stopped when no longer needed.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(69, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'The most effective natural protection against stress ulceration in ICU is:', '["Prolonged fasting before each procedure", "Giving liquid antacids every hour by NG tube", "Keeping the patient nil by mouth until extubation", "Early enteral feeding and good splanchnic perfusion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Gut perfusion and nutrition protect the mucosa.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(70, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'Ranson''s criteria on admission for acute (non-gallstone) pancreatitis include:', '["Serum sodium, potassium and chloride with urine output", "Haemoglobin, platelet count and serum bilirubin at admission", "Age over 55, WBC over 16 000, glucose over 11, raised LDH and AST", "Serum amylase over 3 times normal and a raised serum lipase"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Amylase is not part of Ranson''s criteria; it does not predict severity.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(71, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'Grey Turner''s sign in severe pancreatitis is:', '["A palpable gallbladder", "Bruising of the flanks", "Bruising around the umbilicus", "Yellowing of the sclera"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cullen''s sign is periumbilical bruising; both indicate retroperitoneal haemorrhage.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(72, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'The most important early treatment priority in severe acute pancreatitis is:', '["Early surgery to remove the pancreas", "Strict fasting for 2 weeks", "Goal-directed IV fluid resuscitation", "Prophylactic antibiotics in all cases"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fluids prevent hypovolaemia and necrosis. Early enteral feeding is preferred to TPN.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(73, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'Hypocalcaemia in severe pancreatitis is caused by:', '["Hyperparathyroidism from pancreatic damage", "Saponification of calcium in fat necrosis", "Retention of calcium by the kidneys", "Excess calcium absorption from the gut"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Calcium below 2 mmol/L is a poor prognostic sign; watch for tetany and arrhythmias.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(74, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'The hallmark early sign of necrotising fasciitis is:', '["Itching with small blisters only", "A localised abscess that is fluctuant", "Pain out of proportion to the skin changes", "A painless, slowly spreading red rash"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fascia is destroyed beneath normal-looking skin; crepitus, bullae and anaesthesia appear later.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(75, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'Type II necrotising fasciitis is caused by:', '["Mixed anaerobic and aerobic gut organisms", "Vibrio vulnificus from contact with seawater", "Candida species after broad-spectrum antibiotics", "Group A Streptococcus, often with S. aureus"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Type I is polymicrobial; type III is Gram-negative marine (Vibrio) or Clostridial.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(76, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'The definitive treatment of necrotising fasciitis is:', '["IV antibiotics alone for at least two weeks", "Hyperbaric oxygen therapy alone, daily", "Warm compresses, elevation and analgesia", "Urgent radical debridement with IV antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Delay greatly increases mortality; repeated debridement is often needed.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(77, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'After transurethral resection of the prostate, continuous bladder irrigation output appears bright red with clots. The ICU nurse should:', '["Increase irrigation, check the catheter and inform the surgeon", "Clamp the catheter so that the clots tamponade the bleeding", "Stop the irrigation completely until the bleeding settles", "Remove the catheter and reinsert a smaller one urgently"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bright red output with clots suggests arterial bleeding; traction on the catheter may be applied by the surgeon.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(78, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'A post-TURP patient becomes confused with BP 170/100, HR 50 and sodium 118 mmol/L. This is:', '["Cerebral haemorrhage from the hypertension", "Septic shock from a urinary tract infection", "TURP syndrome from absorbed hypotonic irrigation", "Hypoglycaemia from prolonged pre-op fasting"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Treat with fluid restriction, diuretics and cautious hypertonic saline for severe hyponatraemia.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(79, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'After relief of chronic urinary tract obstruction, the ICU nurse should anticipate:', '["Fluid overload, so IV fluids must be restricted at once", "Post-obstructive diuresis with hypovolaemia and electrolyte loss", "Hyperkalaemia from potassium retention by the damaged kidneys", "Persistent anuria for several days after the catheter is passed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Urine output can exceed 200 mL/hour; replace fluid and monitor electrolytes.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(80, 'Abdominal Compartment Syndrome, Stress Ulcer, Pancreatitis, Fasciitis, Urology', 'A pelvic fracture with blood at the urethral meatus suggests:', '["Bladder infection; urgent urethral catheter is needed", "Urethral injury; avoid urethral catheterisation until imaged", "Renal injury that will need an urgent nephrectomy", "A normal finding after any pelvic fracture"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A retrograde urethrogram is done; a suprapubic catheter may be needed.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(81, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'The main indication for exchange blood transfusion in a newborn is:', '["Mild physiological jaundice on day 3", "Severe jaundice despite intensive phototherapy", "Uncomplicated prematurity below 34 weeks", "Neonatal hypoglycaemia not responding to feeds"]'::jsonb, 1, 'It removes bilirubin and sensitised red cells (e.g. in Rh or ABO haemolytic disease) and corrects anaemia, preventing kernicterus.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(82, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'A double-volume exchange transfusion in a neonate uses about:', '["40–60 mL/kg of blood", "300–350 mL/kg of blood", "80–90 mL/kg of blood", "160–180 mL/kg of blood"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Neonatal blood volume is 80–90 mL/kg; double volume replaces about 85% of the baby''s red cells.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(83, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'During exchange transfusion, blood is removed and replaced in small aliquots mainly to:', '["Allow more bilirubin to be removed", "Avoid sudden swings in blood volume", "Speed up the whole procedure", "Reduce the amount of donor blood used"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Aliquots of 5–20 mL depending on weight are used.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(84, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'A complication of exchange transfusion that the nurse must monitor for is:', '["Polycythaemia from excess donor cells", "Hypercalcaemia from the stored blood", "Persistent hyperglycaemia for days", "Hypocalcaemia from citrate, with jitteriness"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Other complications include hypoglycaemia, hyperkalaemia, thrombocytopenia, NEC and infection.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(85, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'Blood for neonatal exchange transfusion should ideally be:', '["Taken from a parent to reduce reactions", "Over 3 weeks old to reduce potassium", "Fresh, irradiated, CMV-negative and warmed", "Cold from the fridge to reduce bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fresh blood has lower potassium; irradiation prevents graft-versus-host disease.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(86, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'Kernicterus is caused by deposition of unconjugated bilirubin in the:', '["Bone marrow precursors", "Basal ganglia of the brain", "Proximal renal tubules", "Hepatocytes of the liver"]'::jsonb, 1, 'It causes lethargy, hypotonia, opisthotonus and later athetoid cerebral palsy and deafness.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(87, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'In neonatal resuscitation, the most important and effective action is:', '["Giving sodium bicarbonate", "Immediate chest compressions", "Effective ventilation of the lungs", "Giving adrenaline through a UVC"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Most neonatal arrests are respiratory; inflation breaths and ventilation restore the heart rate in most babies.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(88, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'A nurse is performing neonatal resuscitation. The newborn''s heart rate is below 60 beats per minute after 30 seconds of effective positive-pressure ventilation. The next step is to:', '["Administer epinephrine", "Continue positive-pressure ventilation for another 30 seconds", "Initiate chest compressions", "Stop resuscitation and call the physician"]'::jsonb, 2, 'According to NALS guidelines, if the heart rate remains below 60 beats per minute after 30 seconds of effective positive-pressure ventilation, the nurse should initiate chest compressions while continuing ventilation. Epinephrine is administered if the heart rate remains below 60 bpm after another 60 seconds of chest compressions and ventilation.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(89, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'The compression to ventilation ratio in newborn resuscitation is:', '["3:1, about 90 compressions and 30 breaths/min", "15:2, as in older children with two rescuers", "30:2, as in adult basic life support", "5:1, with continuous compressions throughout"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two-thumb encircling technique on the lower third of the sternum is preferred.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(90, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'Neonatal adrenaline dose via umbilical venous catheter is:', '["10–30 micrograms/kg (0.1–0.3 mL/kg of 1:10 000)", "100 micrograms/kg (0.1 mL/kg of 1:1000)", "0.5 mg/kg (0.5 mL/kg of 1:1000)", "1 mg (10 mL of 1:10 000) for any weight"]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is given if the heart rate remains below 60 despite effective ventilation and compressions.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(91, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'Early-onset neonatal sepsis (within 72 hours) is most commonly caused by:', '["Pseudomonas from ventilators", "Staphylococcus epidermidis from lines", "Candida albicans", "Group B Streptococcus and E. coli"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Late-onset sepsis is often hospital-acquired, e.g. coagulase-negative staphylococci.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(92, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'Maternal risk factors for early-onset neonatal sepsis include:', '["Maternal age under 25 years with a first pregnancy", "Planned caesarean section before the onset of labour", "Maternal hypertension that is controlled with drugs", "Membranes ruptured over 18 hours, with maternal fever"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Chorioamnionitis and GBS colonisation also increase the risk.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(93, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'A neonate with sepsis may present with:', '["Hypertension with bradycardia and a loud cry", "Unstable temperature, apnoea, poor feeding, lethargy", "Localised neck stiffness with a bulging fontanelle", "High-grade fever with rigors and sweating only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Signs are non-specific; hypothermia is common in sick newborns.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(94, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'Initial empiric antibiotics for early-onset neonatal sepsis are usually:', '["Ceftriaxone plus metronidazole in all cases", "Vancomycin alone", "Benzylpenicillin (or ampicillin) plus gentamicin", "Oral amoxicillin alone"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ceftriaxone is avoided in jaundiced neonates because it displaces bilirubin from albumin.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(95, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'Daily maintenance fluid for a term neonate on day 1 is about:', '["20 mL/kg/day until the baby passes urine", "150 mL/kg/day from the first hour of life", "60 mL/kg/day, increasing through the first week", "200 mL/kg/day to prevent jaundice"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fluids increase to about 150 mL/kg/day by day 5–7. Preterm and phototherapy babies may need more.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(96, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'The usual IV fluid for neonates in the first 24 hours is:', '["0.9% sodium chloride", "5% albumin", "10% dextrose", "Hartmann''s solution with potassium"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Neonates need glucose (4–6 mg/kg/min). Sodium and potassium are usually added after 24–48 hours.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(97, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'Trophic (minimal enteral) feeding in a sick preterm neonate is given to:', '["Remove the need for any IV fluids at all", "Treat neonatal jaundice by binding bilirubin", "Provide all of the baby''s nutritional needs", "Stimulate gut maturation and improve tolerance"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Small volumes of breast milk (10–20 mL/kg/day) are used, often with parenteral nutrition.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(98, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'Sudden infant death syndrome risk is reduced by:', '["Bed-sharing with parents who smoke at night", "Sleeping prone to prevent aspiration of vomit", "Back to sleep, no smoke exposure, no overheating", "Soft pillows and heavy blankets in the cot"]'::jsonb, 2, '''Back to sleep'' campaigns have greatly reduced SIDS.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(99, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'A premature neonate is at risk of retinopathy of prematurity mainly because of:', '["Feeding with expressed breast milk", "Phototherapy for neonatal jaundice", "High or fluctuating oxygen levels", "Repeated low blood glucose levels"]'::jsonb, 2, 'SpO₂ is targeted (e.g. 90–95%) to avoid hyperoxia.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(100, 'Neonatal Critical Care: Exchange Transfusion, Resuscitation, Sepsis, Feeding', 'Severe neonatal hypothermia increases the risk of:', '["Increased surfactant production", "Hyperglycaemia and metabolic alkalosis", "Hypertension and polyuria", "Hypoglycaemia, acidosis and respiratory distress"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Cold stress increases oxygen use and brown fat metabolism.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(101, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'Mal-rotation with volvulus can be diagnosed through the following EXCEPT:', '["ESR", "History taking", "Abdominal ultrasound", "Abdominal x-ray"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ESR (an inflammatory marker) doesn''t diagnose malrotation/volvulus, unlike imaging and history.', 'Paediatric'),
(102, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'A 9-month-old has paroxysmal colic, drawing up of the legs, vomiting and ''redcurrant jelly'' stools. The first-line treatment if there is no peritonitis is:', '["Antibiotics and observation for 48 hours", "Air or contrast enema reduction under imaging", "Immediate laparotomy and resection", "Oral laxatives"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Enema reduction succeeds in most cases; failure, perforation or peritonitis needs surgery.', 'Paediatric'),
(103, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'Hirschsprung''s disease is diagnosed definitively by:', '["Serum bilirubin and liver function tests", "Stool culture showing enteric organisms", "Plain abdominal X-ray showing dilated loops", "Rectal suction biopsy showing no ganglion cells"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hirschsprung-associated enterocolitis (fever, explosive diarrhoea, distension) is life-threatening.', 'Paediatric'),
(104, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'Bilious vomiting in a newborn requires an urgent:', '["Upper GI contrast study to exclude malrotation", "Stool test for reducing sugars and pH", "Trial of a change of formula milk for 24 hours", "A course of oral antibiotics and review"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Volvulus can cause gangrene of the whole midgut within hours.', 'Paediatric'),
(105, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'A child with perforated appendicitis develops fever and abdominal distension 5 days after surgery. The nurse should suspect:', '["Pelvic or intra-abdominal abscess", "Urinary tract infection only", "Paralytic ileus without infection", "Normal post-operative course"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Persistent fever with pain suggests abscess; ultrasound or CT is needed.', 'Paediatric'),
(106, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'Before surgery for pyloric stenosis, the infant''s blood gas usually shows:', '["Hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis", "Respiratory acidosis from abdominal distension", "Hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis, normal K⁺", "Hyperkalaemic metabolic acidosis with low Na⁺"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Surgery is delayed until fluids and electrolytes are corrected to prevent post-operative apnoea.', 'Paediatric'),
(107, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'Thalassaemia major patients on regular transfusion are at risk of:', '["Iron deficiency anaemia", "Iron overload needing chelation", "Vitamin K excess and bleeding", "Secondary polycythaemia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Iron deposits damage the heart, liver and endocrine glands.', 'Paediatric'),
(108, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'Hb electrophoresis shows HbS and HbC. This patient has:', '["Normal adult haemoglobin", "Sickle cell trait (HbAS) only", "Beta thalassaemia major", "HbSC disease, a sickling disorder"]'::jsonb, 3, 'HbSC disease can cause crises, retinopathy and avascular necrosis, though usually milder than HbSS.', 'Paediatric'),
(109, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'Acute chest syndrome in sickle cell disease presents with:', '["Isolated limb pain without any fever or hypoxia", "Pallor and a rapidly enlarging spleen only", "New lung infiltrate with chest pain, fever, hypoxia", "Haematuria with loin pain and normal oxygen"]'::jsonb, 2, 'It is a leading cause of death; treat with oxygen, analgesia, antibiotics, incentive spirometry and transfusion.', 'Paediatric'),
(110, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'In sickle cell crisis, the ICU nurse should avoid:', '["IV fluids at the maintenance rate", "Hypothermia, dehydration, hypoxia, acidosis", "Opioid analgesia for severe crisis pain", "Oxygen therapy when the SpO₂ is low"]'::jsonb, 1, 'These factors increase sickling and vaso-occlusion.', 'Paediatric'),
(111, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'A child with acute leukaemia and a WBC of 300 × 10⁹/L is at risk of:', '["Secondary polycythaemia and stroke", "Leucostasis and tumour lysis syndrome", "Hypercoagulability only, with DVT", "Iron overload and haemosiderosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hyperhydration, allopurinol or rasburicase and avoiding transfusion until leucocytes fall are key.', 'Paediatric'),
(112, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'Tumour lysis syndrome produces:', '["Low K⁺, low phosphate and high Ca²⁺", "High K⁺, high phosphate, high urate, low Ca²⁺", "Low glucose with high Ca²⁺ and low urate", "Low Na⁺ only, with normal potassium"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Acute kidney injury and arrhythmias can follow.', 'Paediatric'),
(113, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'A febrile neutropenic child (neutrophils below 0.5 × 10⁹/L) needs:', '["Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics within 1 hour", "Live vaccines to boost immunity", "Antibiotics only after culture results", "Oral paracetamol and observation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Febrile neutropenia is an emergency; reverse barrier nursing is used.', 'Paediatric'),
(114, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'Lassa fever is transmitted mainly by:', '["Contaminated beef from infected cattle", "Tick bites in grassland areas", "The multimammate rat and infected body fluids", "Anopheles mosquito bites at night"]'::jsonb, 2, 'It is endemic in Nigeria. Human-to-human spread occurs in hospitals without barrier precautions.', 'Paediatric'),
(115, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'The specific antiviral used for Lassa fever is:', '["Oseltamivir given orally", "Ribavirin, best started early", "Acyclovir given IV 8-hourly", "Zidovudine with lamivudine"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Started within 6 days of illness, it reduces mortality.', 'Paediatric'),
(116, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'A child with suspected Lassa fever is admitted. The nurse''s first priority is:', '["Relatives providing care freely at the bedside", "Admission to an open paediatric ward", "Strict isolation with full PPE", "Isolation only after results return"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Health worker infection is common; sharps and body fluids are handled with extreme care.', 'Paediatric'),
(117, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'Ebola virus disease is transmitted mainly by:', '["Drinking contaminated water supplies only", "Bites from infected mosquitoes at dawn and dusk", "Contact with blood or body fluids of the sick or dead", "Airborne spread over long distances in the community"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Unsafe burial practices and caring for sick relatives spread infection.', 'Paediatric'),
(118, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'In a child with Ebola virus disease, the main supportive treatment is:', '["Routine whole blood transfusion every day", "High-dose steroids and IV antihistamines", "Broad-spectrum antibiotics alone, given IV", "Aggressive fluid and electrolyte replacement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Severe vomiting and diarrhoea cause shock. Monoclonal antibody therapies are now available.', 'Paediatric'),
(119, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'Bacterial meningitis in a child: the priority after suspecting it is:', '["CT scan before any antibiotics in all cases", "Lumbar puncture before any treatment, even if shocked", "Oral antibiotics and observation", "Blood cultures, then IV antibiotics without delay"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Antibiotics must not be delayed by investigations; LP is deferred if ICP is raised or the child is unstable.', 'Paediatric'),
(120, 'Paediatric Surgical Emergencies, Haemoglobinopathies and Viral Haemorrhagic Fevers', 'In bacterial meningitis, dexamethasone is given before or with the first antibiotic dose to reduce:', '["The need for maintenance IV fluids", "The total duration of the antibiotic course", "Fever only, with no effect on outcome", "Hearing loss and neurological complications"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is most effective in Haemophilus influenzae and pneumococcal meningitis.', 'Paediatric'),
(121, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'Polyhydramnios is defined as an amniotic fluid index above:', '["24–25 cm (or deepest pool above 8 cm)", "10 cm (or deepest pool above 3 cm)", "5 cm (or deepest pool above 2 cm)", "15 cm (or deepest pool above 5 cm)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Oligohydramnios is AFI below 5 cm.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(122, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'A recognised cause of polyhydramnios is:', '["Placental insufficiency with growth restriction", "Fetal oesophageal atresia, preventing swallowing", "Fetal renal agenesis, preventing urine output", "Post-term pregnancy beyond 42 weeks"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Maternal diabetes, multiple pregnancy and fetal anomalies are other causes. Renal agenesis causes oligohydramnios.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(123, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'A woman with severe polyhydramnios is at risk during labour of:', '["Uterine hypertonia and fetal hypoxia", "Cord prolapse and postpartum haemorrhage", "Shoulder dystocia and perineal tears", "Prolonged pregnancy and macrosomia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sudden rupture of membranes can cause cord prolapse or abruption; overdistension causes atony.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(124, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'Asymmetrical intra-uterine growth restriction is usually caused by:', '["Placental insufficiency late in pregnancy", "Congenital infection in the first trimester", "Genetic small stature", "Chromosomal abnormalities early in pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Head growth is spared at the expense of the abdomen (''brain sparing'').', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(125, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'An IUGR newborn is at particular risk of:', '["Hypoglycaemia, hypothermia and polycythaemia", "Hyperglycaemia, hyperthermia and anaemia", "Hyperbilirubinaemia only, from haemolysis", "Macrosomia, birth injury and fractures"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Low glycogen stores and fat cause hypoglycaemia and heat loss.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(126, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'Doppler showing absent or reversed end-diastolic flow in the umbilical artery indicates:', '["Normal placental function in late pregnancy", "Fetal macrosomia from maternal diabetes", "Polyhydramnios from excess fetal urine", "Severe placental insufficiency with fetal compromise"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It often leads to urgent delivery.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(127, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'Cervical incompetence typically presents as:', '["Hypertension with heavy proteinuria", "Painful contractions in the first trimester", "Painless dilatation with second-trimester loss", "Bleeding from placenta praevia at term"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Cervical cerclage (e.g. McDonald suture) is placed at 12–14 weeks in high-risk women.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(128, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'A woman with a cervical cerclage goes into labour at 35 weeks. The nurse should:', '["Give tocolytics and keep the stitch", "Leave the suture until delivery", "Arrange urgent removal of the suture", "Start oxytocin infusion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Labour against a stitch can tear the cervix or rupture the uterus.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(129, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'Puerperal sepsis is infection of the genital tract occurring:', '["From membrane rupture or labour to 42 days postpartum", "Only after the 6-week postnatal check", "Only in the first 24 hours after delivery", "Only during the first stage of labour"]'::jsonb, 0, 'It remains a major cause of maternal death in Nigeria.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(130, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'The most dangerous organism causing puerperal sepsis is:', '["Staphylococcus epidermidis from the skin", "Group A Streptococcus (S. pyogenes)", "Lactobacillus species from the vagina", "Candida albicans after antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 1, 'It can cause rapid toxic shock; early IV antibiotics and source control are vital.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(131, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'A postpartum woman has fever 38.9 °C, HR 125, BP 85/50, foul lochia and confusion. The nurse should immediately:', '["Encourage breastfeeding to help the uterus contract", "Sepsis bundle: cultures, IV antibiotics within 1 hour, fluids", "Oral paracetamol and a review of the observations in 4 hours", "Oxytocin infusion and hourly observation only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Use MEOWS; septic shock may need vasopressors and source control (e.g. evacuation of retained products).', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(132, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'Risk factors for puerperal sepsis include:', '["Multiparity with a quick spontaneous vaginal birth", "A short labour with intact membranes until delivery", "Caesarean section, long labour, retained products, many VEs", "An elective delivery with no vaginal examinations"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Anaemia, diabetes and poor hygiene also increase risk.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(133, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'Supine hypotensive syndrome in late pregnancy is relieved by:', '["A steep head-down (Trendelenburg) position", "Raising both legs on pillows while supine", "Left lateral tilt or manual uterine displacement", "Lying flat on the back with arms by the side"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gravid uterus compresses the IVC and aorta, reducing venous return and cardiac output.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(134, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'During CPR in a woman over 20 weeks pregnant, the team should:', '["Avoid defibrillation altogether because of the fetal heart", "Displace the uterus left and prepare for perimortem caesarean by 4–5 min", "Use a slower compression rate to protect the pregnant uterus", "Delay CPR until the baby has been delivered by caesarean section"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Delivery improves maternal venous return and resuscitation success.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(135, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'Hypotension after spinal anaesthesia for caesarean section is best treated with:', '["Phenylephrine infusion with fluid co-loading", "Large doses of IV furosemide and fluid restriction", "Withholding all IV fluids to avoid oedema", "Head-up tilt to 45 degrees with the legs down"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Phenylephrine maintains BP without fetal acidosis; ephedrine is an alternative.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(136, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'Amniotic fluid embolism classically presents with:', '["Sudden collapse, hypoxia, hypotension and DIC", "A slowly rising blood pressure", "Gradual onset of fever and a rash", "Headache and visual disturbance only"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Management is supportive: airway, circulation, blood products and delivery.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(137, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'The Modified Early Obstetric Warning Score (MEOWS) is used to:', '["Interpret fetal heart rate patterns in labour", "Assess the maturity of the newborn at birth", "Grade perineal tears after vaginal delivery", "Detect deterioration in pregnant and postnatal women"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It uses obstetric-specific thresholds for vital signs.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(138, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'Hyperemesis gravidarum may lead to which serious neurological complication?', '["Guillain-Barré syndrome after infection", "Bell''s palsy from dehydration", "Wernicke''s encephalopathy (thiamine deficiency)", "Myasthenia gravis triggered by vomiting"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Thiamine is given before dextrose-containing fluids.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(139, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'In severe pre-eclampsia, magnesium sulphate toxicity is first detected by:', '["A rising heart rate", "Loss of the patellar reflex", "An increasing urine output", "A rising blood pressure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Respiratory depression and cardiac arrest follow at higher levels; calcium gluconate is the antidote.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(140, 'Obstetric Critical Care: Polyhydramnios, IUGR, Cervical Incompetence, Puerperal Sepsis', 'Postpartum DIC is most commonly associated with:', '["Mild iron-deficiency anaemia during pregnancy", "Uncomplicated breastfeeding in the first week", "Normal vaginal delivery at term without problems", "Placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism, sepsis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Fibrinogen, platelets and clotting are monitored; blood products are replaced.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(141, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'Active euthanasia differs from withdrawal of futile treatment because euthanasia:', '["It must always be requested by the family", "It involves stopping a ventilator after brain death", "It allows the disease to take its natural course", "It intentionally ends life by a direct act, which is illegal"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Withdrawing futile treatment with comfort care is ethically and legally accepted; deliberately killing is not.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(142, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'A conscious, competent ICU patient asks the nurse to give a lethal dose of morphine. The nurse should:', '["Give the dose, because the patient''s autonomy must be respected", "Ask the relatives to decide whether the dose should be given", "Refuse, explore the suffering and get the team to improve symptom control", "Give a smaller dose secretly so that the patient feels comforted"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Euthanasia is illegal; the nurse addresses pain, depression and fears and may involve palliative care.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(143, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'A dying declaration is:', '["A statement by someone who believes death is imminent about the cause", "A written will made by a healthy person before any illness", "A request by the patient to withdraw life support", "A certificate issued by a doctor confirming the death"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In court it may be accepted as evidence; the nurse records it exactly, with time and witnesses.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(144, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'When an assault victim in ICU makes a dying declaration, the nurse should:', '["Ignore it because only the police may record it", "Record the exact words, the time and witnesses, and sign", "Ask leading questions to obtain more details", "Summarise it in her own words at the end of the shift"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Accuracy and witnessing make it admissible.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(145, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'The ''principle of double effect'' supports giving high-dose opioids to a dying patient when:', '["The intention is to relieve suffering, even if death is hastened", "The ICU bed is urgently needed for another patient", "The intention is to shorten the patient''s life", "The family has asked for a quicker death"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Intention and proportionality distinguish palliative care from euthanasia.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(146, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'A Do-Not-Attempt-Resuscitation order means:', '["Only the family may give medications", "All treatment, including feeding, is stopped", "The patient is discharged from ICU at once", "No CPR, but all other appropriate care continues"]'::jsonb, 3, 'DNAR does not mean ''do not treat''.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(147, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'The Patient''s Bill of Rights includes the right to:', '["Demanding any treatment regardless of clinical need", "Information, consent or refusal, privacy and respect", "Choosing the nurses'' shift patterns and rotas", "Reading other patients'' records on request"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Rights are balanced with professional judgement and resources.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(148, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'The following are among the patients'' bill of rights EXCEPT:', '["Right to dignity and personality", "Right to prompt attention and attendance", "Right to get married before discharge", "Right to access care when and as due"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The patients'' bill of rights covers prompt attention, dignity and access to care. Getting married before discharge is not a patient right.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(149, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'Res ipsa loquitur applies when:', '["The injury was caused only by the natural progression of the patient''s disease", "The injury would not occur without negligence and the defendant controlled the cause", "The nurse followed all the written protocols and the injury was unavoidable", "The patient signed a written consent form before the procedure was carried out"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Examples include a swab left in the abdomen or a burn from a diathermy plate.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(150, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'A nurse who helps at a road accident while off duty is protected under Good Samaritan principles if she:', '["Performs procedures she has never been trained to do", "Acts in good faith, within competence, without gross negligence", "Leaves the casualty halfway through care to go home", "Charges a reasonable fee for the help given at the scene"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Once help is started, it should be continued until care is handed over.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(151, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'A patient bitten by a dog of unknown vaccination status arrives in the emergency unit. The nurse''s legal and clinical duty includes:', '["Waiting for the dog to die before giving any treatment", "Wound care, rabies PEP and reporting the bite", "Treating only if the dog''s owner agrees to pay", "Discharge without treatment if the wound is small"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Animal bites are notifiable; rabies is almost always fatal once symptoms start.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(152, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'Rabies post-exposure prophylaxis for a severe (category III) bite includes:', '["Wound washing, rabies vaccine and immunoglobulin", "Tetanus toxoid only, with a booster", "Oral antibiotics only for seven days", "Observation of the dog for 10 days only"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Immediate wound washing with soap and water for 15 minutes reduces risk.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(153, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'The Code of Professional Conduct for nurses in Nigeria is issued by:', '["The Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria", "The Federal Ministry of Health, Abuja", "The hospital''s management board", "The Medical and Dental Council of Nigeria"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Breach may lead to disciplinary action, suspension or removal from the register.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(154, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'In a suspected criminal case, a bullet removed from a patient in ICU should be:', '["Labelled, sealed and given to police with chain of custody", "Washed and handed to the relatives on request", "Discarded with the clinical waste in theatre", "Kept by the surgeon in a pot as a souvenir"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Proper handling preserves evidence for court.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(155, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'Fidelity in nursing ethics means:', '["Distributing resources fairly", "Telling the truth at all times", "Keeping promises and commitments", "Avoiding harm to the patient"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Veracity is truth-telling; non-maleficence is avoiding harm.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(156, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'A nurse documents care after the patient''s death that was not actually given. This is:', '["Good documentation practice in ICU", "Required by law to complete the chart", "Falsification of records and misconduct", "Acceptable if the care had been planned"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Late entries must be clearly dated and labelled as such.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(157, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'Informed consent for an ICU procedure is not valid if:', '["The patient was coerced or not told material risks", "A witness was present at the signing", "The patient asked questions before signing", "The doctor explained the alternatives"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Valid consent must be voluntary, informed and given by a competent person.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(158, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'When a non-responsive on-call doctor fails to attend a deteriorating patient, the nurse should:', '["Escalate through the chain of command, documenting calls", "Transfer the patient to another hospital herself", "Wait until the consultant''s morning ward round", "Treat the patient beyond her scope without orders"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Documentation protects the patient and the nurse.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(159, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'Advance directives made by a competent patient should be:', '["Respected when valid and applicable", "Overruled by the relatives'' wishes", "Ignored once the patient is unconscious", "Followed only if the doctor agrees"]'::jsonb, 0, 'They express the patient''s autonomy for future care.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(160, 'Ethico-legal Issues in Critical Care', 'Vicarious liability means that:', '["The employer may be liable for staff negligence at work", "The patient is liable for the errors made by staff", "Nurses are never personally accountable for errors", "Only the employer can ever be sued for negligence"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nurses remain accountable to the NMCN for their own practice.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(161, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'The level of ICU that possess an active research programme with dedicated research staff is:', '["Level 1", "Level 11", "Level 1V", "Level 111"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A dedicated research programme is a feature of the highest (Level III) ICU classification.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(162, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'The ICU manager knows that some of her staffs prefers to be directed rather than to be allowed to use initiative or to exercise responsibility. This statement implies:', '["Theory x", "Theory A", "Theory b", "Theory y"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Preferring direction over independent initiative reflects Theory X assumptions about workers.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(163, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'An ICU team designs a new sedation-weaning protocol, trials it on one shift for a week, then reviews compliance and outcome data before deciding whether to adopt it unit-wide. This is an example of:', '["A retrospective nursing audit", "A root cause analysis of an incident", "A PDSA quality improvement cycle", "A risk financing strategy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Small-scale trial, data review, and decision on wider adoption is the hallmark of the Plan-Do-Study-Act improvement cycle.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(164, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'Which of these is an example of a structure standard in a critical care unit QA programme?', '["Nurse documents pain score every 2 hours", "Ratio of one nurse to two ventilated patients", "Patient reports satisfaction with pain control", "Reduction in ventilator-associated pneumonia rate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Staffing ratios are a resource/structural element of care, classified as a structure standard.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(165, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'A QA audit reveals that although the ICU has adequate equipment and staff (structure) and nurses are documenting care appropriately (process), patient outcomes remain poor. This scenario illustrates that:', '["Structure and process alone are insufficient; outcomes must be measured", "The audit should be stopped because the standards are met", "Outcome measures are irrelevant when the structure is adequate", "Good structure and process always guarantee good patient outcomes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Adequate structure and process do not guarantee good outcomes, which is why outcome measurement remains essential in evaluating overall quality.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(166, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'A Level 3 critical care patient is one who needs:', '["Routine post-operative care after minor surgery", "Advanced respiratory support, or support of two or more organs", "Observation more frequent than is possible on the ward", "Single organ support such as non-invasive ventilation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Level 2 (HDU) patients need single-organ support or step-down care.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(167, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'The recommended nurse-to-patient ratio for a ventilated Level 3 patient is:', '["1:2", "1:4", "1:1", "1:6"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Level 2 patients usually need 1:2 nursing.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(168, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'The most appropriate use of an ICU admission policy is to:', '["Admit only post-operative surgical patients", "Keep some beds empty for private patients", "Admit patients strictly in order of arrival", "Give beds to patients most likely to benefit"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Triage considers severity, reversibility and expected benefit.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(169, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'An ICU outreach (rapid response) team mainly aims to:', '["Replace ward nursing staff during staff shortages", "Discharge ICU patients faster to free beds", "Perform elective procedures on the wards", "Identify and treat deteriorating ward patients early"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Early warning scores trigger outreach review, reducing cardiac arrests and late ICU admissions.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(170, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'The APACHE II score is used in ICU to:', '["Calculate nursing workload for the duty roster", "Assess the depth of sedation in ventilated patients", "Measure pain in patients who cannot self-report", "Estimate illness severity and predicted mortality"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It uses physiological values in the first 24 hours, age and chronic health.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(171, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'The SOFA score assesses dysfunction in:', '["Nutritional status, using albumin and weight", "Only the brain, using the GCS and the pupils", "Only the lungs and kidneys, as in ARDS with AKI", "Six organs: lung, heart, liver, clotting, kidney, CNS"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An increase of 2 or more points with infection defines sepsis (Sepsis-3).', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(172, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'The best method to distribute ICU nurses on a shift is according to:', '["Patient acuity and nurse competence", "Personal preference of nurses", "Alphabetical order of names", "Seniority of the nurses only"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dependency or acuity scoring tools support safe allocation.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(173, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'In the design of an ICU, each bed space should have:', '["Space, oxygen, air, suction and several power outlets", "No hand-washing basin, to save floor space", "Oxygen outlets shared with the next bed space", "A single power socket for all the equipment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Isolation rooms, central monitoring and a clear view of patients are also needed.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(174, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'An ICU discharge to the ward should ideally occur:', '["During the day with a structured handover", "Without notes to save time", "At night to free beds quickly", "Before the ward has accepted the patient"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Night discharges are associated with higher readmission and mortality.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(175, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'An ICU manager notices rising central line infections. The best first step is:', '["Give antibiotic prophylaxis to all patients", "Ban the use of central lines in the unit", "Audit compliance with the central line bundle", "Discipline the nurse who was on duty"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Bundle audits identify gaps in practice to target education.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(176, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'Equipment management in ICU includes:', '["Allowing untrained staff to set up ventilators", "Repairing equipment only when it fails in use", "Planned maintenance, inventory and staff training", "Keeping faulty equipment at the bedside for spares"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each device should have a service record and user competency.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(177, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'Burnout among ICU nurses is best reduced by:', '["Removing teaching sessions to reduce workload", "Ignoring signs of distress to keep morale up", "Adequate staffing, breaks, debriefing and support", "Longer shifts with fewer days off each month"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Moral distress and compassion fatigue are common in ICU.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(178, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'The ICU budget is mainly used to plan for:', '["Only the salaries of the ICU nursing staff", "Only food and catering for the patients", "Only buying new buildings and furniture", "Staffing, consumables, drugs and equipment upkeep"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Staff costs form the largest part of ICU costs.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(179, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'An open ICU differs from a closed ICU in that, in an open ICU:', '["Patients can walk in freely without any referral or review", "Only the intensivists may write prescriptions for patients", "There are no doors or walls separating the bed spaces", "Patients stay under their admitting consultants, not an intensivist"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Closed units led by intensivists are associated with better outcomes.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(180, 'ICU Management, Organisation and Leadership', 'After a cardiac arrest in ICU, a team debrief is mainly held to:', '["Review what went well and what to improve, and support staff", "Prepare a statement to inform the media about the event", "Replace the need for an incident report on the arrest", "Identify the person to blame for the cardiac arrest"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Structured debriefs improve future resuscitation and staff wellbeing.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(181, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'The chemical injury in Mendelson''s syndrome primarily results from which mechanism?', '["Mechanical obstruction of the trachea by food particles alone", "Immune complex deposition in the pulmonary vascular bed", "Acid damage to the alveolar-capillary membrane, causing leaky oedema", "Pulmonary arterial vasospasm triggered by the low pH"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Acidic gastric contents directly damage the alveolar-capillary membrane, increasing capillary permeability and causing a non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema/ARDS-like picture.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(182, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'A patient aspirates acidic gastric contents during an emergency intubation. Within hours, the most likely picture is:', '["A normal chest X-ray with no respiratory symptoms at all", "A unilateral pleural effusion with a shift of the trachea", "Wheeze, hypoxaemia and bilateral infiltrates (chemical pneumonitis)", "Lobar consolidation with a high fever within the first hour"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Mendelson''s syndrome is chemical; bacterial pneumonia may follow later.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(183, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'Prevention of Mendelson''s syndrome in an emergency caesarean section includes:', '["Antacids, H2 blockers, RSI and cricoid pressure", "A large meal two hours before surgery", "Inhalational induction with a face mask", "Avoiding pre-oxygenation to save time"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pregnant women have delayed gastric emptying and lax lower oesophageal sphincter.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(184, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'Anaesthetic deaths are reported and reviewed mainly to:', '["Avoid informing the family about the cause of the death", "Identify and punish the anaesthetist responsible for the death", "Delay the death certificate until the review is complete", "Learn lessons and prevent deaths, with medico-legal accountability"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Confidential enquiries identify system and individual factors.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(185, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'The Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) classifies nutritional status using:', '["Blood glucose and HbA1c measured on the first ICU day", "History (weight loss, intake, GI symptoms, function) plus examination", "Body mass index alone, calculated from height and weight", "Serum albumin alone, measured on admission and weekly"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Patients are graded A (well nourished), B (moderate) or C (severely malnourished).', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(186, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'A patient with hepatic encephalopathy needs nutrition. The current approach is:', '["Fasting until the encephalopathy has resolved fully", "Complete protein restriction until the ammonia normalises", "Normal protein, with branched-chain amino acids if intolerant", "A high-fat, carbohydrate-free diet to reduce ammonia"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Protein restriction worsens malnutrition; lactulose and rifaximin manage ammonia.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(187, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'A patient on CRRT (dialysis) in ICU needs:', '["More protein, as amino acids are lost in the effluent", "Potassium supplements added to every feed", "Very low protein to reduce the urea production", "No nutrition at all until dialysis has stopped"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Protein needs may reach 1.5–2.5 g/kg/day.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(188, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'Refeeding syndrome after starting feeds in a malnourished ICU patient causes:', '["Low phosphate, low potassium and low magnesium", "High calcium, high sodium and high chloride", "High blood glucose with normal electrolytes", "High phosphate, high potassium and high urea"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Start feeds slowly, give thiamine and monitor electrolytes.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(189, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'Inspiratory muscle training in a patient who is difficult to wean aims to:', '["Strengthen the diaphragm and aid weaning", "Reduce cough strength to rest the lungs", "Replace the need for ventilator settings", "Increase the patient''s sedation needs"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Threshold devices give resistance to inspiration for set periods.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(190, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'Incentive spirometry after thoracic or abdominal surgery mainly prevents:', '["Urinary retention", "Atelectasis and pneumonia", "Pressure ulcers", "Deep vein thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Slow, deep, sustained inspiration re-expands alveoli.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(191, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS) in critical care is used to:', '["Defibrillate the heart in ventricular fibrillation", "Pace the phrenic nerve to replace ventilation", "Measure muscle strength in ICU-acquired weakness", "Relieve pain by stimulating large nerve fibres"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It reduces analgesic needs, e.g. after thoracotomy; avoid over pacemakers.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(192, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'Which condition is a well-established indication for therapeutic plasma exchange?', '["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)", "Simple dehydration", "Uncomplicated urinary tract infection", "Mild hyperkalemia"]'::jsonb, 0, 'TTP is a classic indication for urgent plasma exchange, which removes large von Willebrand factor multimers and autoantibodies against ADAMTS13 while replacing the deficient enzyme via donor plasma.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(193, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'A common electrolyte complication of plasmapheresis, related to the citrate anticoagulant used in the extracorporeal circuit, is:', '["Hypernatraemia from the replacement fluid", "Hyperkalaemia from haemolysis in the circuit", "Hyperphosphataemia from cell breakdown", "Hypocalcaemia (citrate toxicity), with perioral tingling"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Citrate used to anticoagulate the apheresis circuit chelates calcium, and some enters the systemic circulation, commonly causing symptomatic hypocalcemia such as perioral or extremity paresthesia and cramps; calcium supplementation is often given prophylactically or as needed.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(194, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'After renal transplant, a sudden fall in urine output with graft tenderness and fever suggests:', '["Over-hydration from excess IV fluids", "Hypoglycaemia caused by the steroids", "Acute rejection or graft vessel thrombosis", "A normal post-operative recovery pattern"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Urgent ultrasound, Doppler and biopsy may be needed.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(195, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'A renal transplant recipient on tacrolimus is at particular risk of:', '["Weight loss and persistent hypotension", "Infection, nephrotoxicity and hyperglycaemia", "Increased antibody production and allergy", "Hypercoagulability from a strong immune system"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Drug levels are monitored; interactions with antifungals and antibiotics are common.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(196, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'In toxic epidermal necrolysis, more than 30% of the body surface is detached. ICU care is similar to:', '["Major burns: fluids, warmth, wound care, infection control, nutrition", "Cellulitis: oral antibiotics and elevation of the limbs", "A simple allergy: antihistamines and observation on the ward", "Eczema: emollients and topical steroids at home"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Stop the culprit drug. SCORTEN predicts mortality.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(197, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'Hospital-acquired infections in ICU are reduced most by:', '["Changing all IV lines every day", "Hand hygiene and care bundles", "Routine broad-spectrum antibiotics for all", "Keeping the windows open for fresh air"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Barrier nursing and environmental cleaning also matter.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(198, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'A key principle of adult learning (andragogy) when teaching ICU staff is that adults:', '["Do not need any feedback on their performance", "Prefer lectures with no participation or discussion", "Learn best when content is relevant and builds on experience", "Learn best by memorising facts without any context"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Knowles described self-direction, experience and problem-centred learning.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(199, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'The ''politics of health care'' in ICU management refers to:', '["Elections held to choose the ward manager", "Nurses joining and campaigning for political parties", "How power, resources and decisions are negotiated", "Government salary scales for health workers only"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nurse managers must advocate effectively for staffing and resources.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care'),
(200, 'Anaesthesia, Nutrition, Physiotherapy, Transplant, Infection and Teaching', 'Reusable ventilator circuits and laryngoscope blades in ICU must undergo at least:', '["Wiping with a dry cloth after each use", "Cleaning, then high-level disinfection or sterilisation", "Rinsing with tap water between each patient", "Low-level disinfection with soap and water only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Items touching mucous membranes are semi-critical (Spaulding). Single-use items must not be reprocessed.', 'Anaesthesia & Respiratory Care');
